{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 46, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-122b-a10b", "content": "Raymond III was a prominent nobleman during the twelfth century who held the title of Count of Tripoli. His life was defined by political maneuvering and military conflict within the Crusader states of the Levant. He served as regent for the young King Baldwin IV of Jerusalem for many years before facing significant challenges from rival factions. His tenure involved complex relationships with the Byzantine Empire and various Muslim leaders including Saladin. Raymond struggled to maintain stability in his own county while also trying to guide the kingdom through a period of increasing tension with external threats.\n\n**Early Life and Succession**\nRaymond inherited the County of Tripoli after the death of his father Raymond II. His succession was not without controversy because his father had been assassinated by the Hashashin sect. Raymond III had to navigate a dangerous political landscape immediately upon taking power. He spent his early years consolidating his authority against local barons and external enemies. His marriage to Eschiva of Bures helped secure his position within the region. This union brought him additional lands and influence that strengthened his rule over the northern coastal territories.\n\n**Regency and Political Struggles**\nThe death of King Amalric in 1174 placed Raymond in a position of immense power as regent for the leprous boy king Baldwin IV. He governed the kingdom with a focus on stability and diplomacy. Raymond opposed the aggressive policies of Raynald of Châtillon who sought to provoke war with Saladin. The Count of Tripoli believed that maintaining peace was essential for the survival of the Crusader states. He tried to arrange a marriage between Baldwin and a Byzantine princess to secure an alliance but these plans failed due to court intrigue.\n\n**The Battle of Hattin and Death**\nTensions between Raymond and Guy of Lusignan eventually led to a civil war within the kingdom. Raymond allied with Saladin against Guy in a desperate attempt to remove him from power. This decision proved fatal for the Latin Kingdom as it fractured their defenses just before a major Muslim offensive. The united front of the Crusaders collapsed at the Battle of Hattin in 1187. Raymond managed to escape the battlefield but died shortly after in Tripoli. His death marked the end of an era for the County of Tripoli and left the region vulnerable to future conquests. His legacy remains a subject of debate among historians regarding his role in the fall of Jerusalem.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.0015299694}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -3.3378658e-06}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.0047387527}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.08697208}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.007218723}, {"token": " prominent", "logprob": -0.914726}, {"token": " noble", "logprob": -0.030557439}, {"token": "man", "logprob": -0.07586693}, {"token": " during", "logprob": -2.63185}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -4.768373e-07}, {"token": " tw", "logprob": -0.17043146}, {"token": "elfth", "logprob": -1.6689314e-06}, {"token": " century", "logprob": -7.8323574e-05}, {"token": " who", "logprob": -0.013841737}, {"token": " held", "logprob": -1.6194668}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.010922319}, {"token": " title", "logprob": -0.0019250552}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.000803376}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -5.9487204e-05}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -4.768373e-07}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -7.15256e-07}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.3510201}, {"token": " His", "logprob": -1.6436937}, {"token": " life", "logprob": -0.018064985}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.1445077}, {"token": " defined", "logprob": -0.8541537}, {"token": " by", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " political", "logprob": -1.6625192}, {"token": " maneuver", "logprob": -0.28347713}, {"token": "ing", "logprob": -